00:04:44.187 --> 00:04:45.770
And I want to note,
too, that offering

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:48.500
an acknowledgment like this,
through the data clouds

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:52.280
and cables that we're
talking through,

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.620
is also to recognize
all of the computer

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:59.720
models of communication that
[INAUDIBLE] the unfolding

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.130
of our conversation itself.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:07.030
So Unfolding-- this
is a panel about how

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:10.480
tools and computations shape
the models that scientists

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:13.930
and artists make of the
universe, the world,

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:16.250
and of models themselves.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:19.870
And I want to revisit and frame
some of the arguments delivered

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:22.570
by our panelists,
whom, if I can,

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:26.230
I would like to have
join me on the screen.

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:28.240
I think there's magic
that can make that happen.

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:34.000
So just to refresh
folks' memories

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.620
about our presentations and
set us up for discussion,

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.890
Priya Natarajan
told us how she uses

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:45.460
computer simulations to
explore the black hole growth

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:48.130
problem in cosmology.

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:52.540
With simulations understood as
what she very usefully calls,

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:55.570
playing a mediatory
role between theory

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:59.350
and observation--
a mediatory role.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:02.200
And more as
potentially generative

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:06.070
of new explanation, new
evidence, new knowledge,

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:07.700
and prediction.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.210
So she argued that
simulations may

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:14.320
be approached as a kind
of creolized language

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:17.590
for communicating in domains
that are simultaneously

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:22.540
experimental, theoretical, and
observational, so simulations

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:26.560
become tools for signification
that may be understood as what

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:28.270
she-- as activating
what she calls

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:32.680
an interglot, a
mix of languages.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:38.890
An interglot-- this is one
of our key words, interglot.

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:42.400
Dr. Ogbunu took the analytic
of unfolding in his paper

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.010
and really ran with it,
making, first, the connection

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:48.040
to protein folding
and protein unfolding,

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:50.740
and then to a really, I
think, inspired connection

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:55.900
to unfolding as disentangling
via Darwin's image,

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:58.900
famous image of the tangled
bank, which then also

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.720
immediately got us to the
questions of the entanglement

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:06.100
of science and society--

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:09.830
genetically modified organisms,
gene editing, et cetera.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:15.040
And he argued that
disentangling biological process

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.670
requires models.

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:21.210
Model's the central dogma
in molecular biology--

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:25.140
DNA makes RNA makes protein--

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:26.640
and models that
also have to operate

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.740
at the right level
for the question

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:30.180
that they're answering.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:34.470
So a model of the COVID-19
pandemic, for example,

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:37.740
will be operating at various
levels for different purposes.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:41.700
Maybe a level of
protein expression,

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.250
maybe to a level of
population dynamics,

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:46.200
maybe to a level
that embraces things

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:49.410
to do with social inequality
and structural racism.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:55.510
And he got us to think about
how models as instruments,

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:58.860
and also, I think, importantly,
as structures of analogy,

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:04.020
so disentangling analogies.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:08.160
We want to think about
how models help us express

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:11.130
analogies, but also
demand that we disentangle

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:15.180
the analogies that we use.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:16.680
Yeah, which analogies do we use?

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.140
I mean, is evolution a game?

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:22.290
Is evolution an algorithm?

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:24.390
Those kinds of questions
go into deciding

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:26.950
how to model what it is.

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:31.840
And Rosa Menkman tells us that
our media, the media on which

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:37.360
we do computer simulations, come
to us not only with promises

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:42.460
to represent, replicate,
record, and extend

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:45.100
sense experience of the
world, but also come

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:48.410
to us in a range of formats--

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:52.618
cassette tapes,
A4 paper, JPEGs--

00:08:52.618 --> 00:08:54.410
but also in [? material. ?]
They come to us

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:58.370
in material manifestations
which have their own limits

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:02.270
and their own affordances
in the resolutions

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:04.640
that they can offer
us, resolutions

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:08.420
that are then animated through
the kinds of compression

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:12.980
protocols that are used for
relaying something like an MP3

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:19.820
or JPEG, and that can glitch
in really interesting ways

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:23.990
that are sometimes disturbing,
sometimes inspiring, sometimes

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:25.310
productive.

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:27.470
And so I think,
when I'm supposed--

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:29.270
I should have one of
these for this, too.

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:30.755
Here we go, compression glitch.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.990
I also have this
book, Glitch Feminism.

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:42.450
There's all kinds of
glitches around my desk.

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:46.980
So we learn through all of
this that computer models

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:49.560
are interglots.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:51.510
They are instruments.

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:55.290
They're analogical
machines, and they are also

00:09:55.290 --> 00:10:01.620
dependent upon standards which
are technical and political,

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:05.610
standards that demand particular
kinds of compressions,

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:13.910
and that produce
artifacts in two senses--

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:17.980
artifacts as things, and
then artifacts as things

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.040
you don't want.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:22.990
And I guess we want to
talk about all of that.

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:25.630
The way that I want to organize
this conversation, just

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:26.860
to start us off--

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:31.030
and we'll also be taking
questions from the audience

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:31.570
later on.

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:34.990
But just to start us off, I want
to take each of our panelists

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:39.370
in turn and pose to them
questions that are inspired

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:43.070
by the other panelists.

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:46.630
So if I can, I'd love
to turn to you, Priya,

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:50.080
to talk about this really
interesting work you're

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:52.810
doing on the black hole
growth problem in cosmology

00:10:52.810 --> 00:11:00.580
and thinking about that through
simulations that mean to match,

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:03.790
in scale, the observational
data that you have

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:10.360
from various telescopes and
how they do that using machine

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:13.240
learning algorithms.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:18.040
But I guess, after watching
Brandon's presentation, which

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:20.200
offers that models may
be seen as instruments,

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:23.920
I wondered whether you
thought of interglots,

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:25.330
about which you can say more--

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:27.580
whether you thought of
inerglots as, themselves, kinds

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:30.400
of instruments?

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:32.320
So maybe do that?

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:33.550
Pick that up, if you like.

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:36.340
Thanks, Stefan.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:37.690
Thanks.

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:43.770
I think I want to,
first, clarify that

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:46.860
when I am talking
about simulations,

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:51.630
I am setting these cosmological
simulations apart in many ways

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:56.250
compared to other ways,
numerically visualizing data

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:59.230
and processes and
equations and so on.

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:04.770
And the reason for that, as
I've argued in the presentation,

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:08.610
was that cosmology is
a very peculiar field.

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:11.910
We cannot perform any kind
of controlled experiments,

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:14.100
laboratory experiment
or whatever.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.780
What you have in the
universe is what you get.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:20.260
So we visually record
and make observations,

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:22.530
and then we have
to interpret them

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:26.220
in the context of
some conceptual model.

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:29.730
And these conceptual models
used to be paper and pencil

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:32.340
equations, and we're
also very lucky that we

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:35.610
have the ordered
physical laws that

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:39.267
give us a structure and a
frame to do the interpretation.

00:12:39.267 --> 00:12:41.100
And increasingly, when
you can't do anything

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:44.250
on pencil and paper, you've
needed computer simulations,

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:46.920
and so that's how
simulations were originally

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:52.710
seen as being merely
enhancing and not generative

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:54.010
of new discoveries.

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:57.690
I mean, also, there's the
philosophical awkwardness

00:12:57.690 --> 00:12:59.520
that you are sitting
inside the universe

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.850
that you are observing, so there
isn't this traditional distance

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:05.880
between the observer
and the observed, other

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:09.120
than the quantum
funniness aside.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:13.080
In classical physics, that is
a very important assumption

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:15.510
because that's tied in with
other [INAUDIBLE] concepts

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:17.307
like objectivity and
so on and so forth.

00:13:17.307 --> 00:13:19.890
So here we are-- we're sitting
in the middle of this universe.

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:21.030
We're making observations.

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:23.160
And there is only one
universe, so I can't actually

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:24.510
replicate it either.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:29.100
So in many ways, cosmology's
not really a science

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:34.095
as traditionally defined,
and therefore, simulations

00:13:34.095 --> 00:13:36.270
play a very important role.

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:42.490
And so I've argued that they
play the mediatory role of--

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:46.650
and they are an interglot
because they are actually

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:51.330
universal in the sense
that, now, simulations

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:57.630
allow you to connect models and
theories to observational data

00:13:57.630 --> 00:13:59.850
directly, except there
are some limitations.

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:02.220
As you pointed out, the
fact that we are not

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:05.280
able to replicate,
in our simulations,

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:08.610
the exact survey volumes
that are observationally

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:10.950
accessible to us with our
telescopes at the moment,

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:13.270
and this is where machine
learning comes in.

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:17.430
And so there have been two
instances, only two instances,

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:19.380
where simulations
have actually been

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:21.340
generative of new knowledge.

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:23.400
One is a very
interesting instance,

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:25.650
which is the firm
Double Negative that

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:29.160
did the visualization for
the movie Interstellar.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:31.650
So they actually saw
Einstein's [INAUDIBLE],,

00:14:31.650 --> 00:14:33.720
and what they
found, they found--

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:36.120
and [INAUDIBLE],, who has
been working in this field,

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:40.650
a Nobel laureate, one of the big
figures in understanding black

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:41.820
hole physics--

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:44.460
he noticed, in
the visualization,

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:47.280
that there was a
non-linearity that he had not

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:48.370
picked up before.

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:50.870
So there's actually a scientific
paper on which people from

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:52.120
Double Negative have authored.

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:54.090
So that's the one
case that I really

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:57.540
know where a simulation
has been generative,

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:01.260
something that was completely
unanticipated, unseen before.

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:03.090
And the second
example is the one

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:09.150
that I gushed on about in
my talk, where we recently

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:11.400
realized, from just
looking at simulations,

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:14.310
that there ought to be a very
large population of wandering

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:19.500
black holes in the universe,
and a handful, one or two,

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:22.118
have been found
because, normally, we

00:15:22.118 --> 00:15:23.910
expect all these black
holes to be anchored

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:27.240
to the centers of galaxies,
like our own galaxy harbors

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:29.070
a dormant black hole, and so on.

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:33.150
So I think the relationship
between models,

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:35.940
speaking to Brandon's work--

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:40.890
so this relationship between
models and observational data

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:45.690
for cosmology is somewhat
apart and slightly different.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:50.430
And I would also kind of clarify
that it's not quite a creole.

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:52.140
It is like a universal
glot, so it's

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:53.640
an interglot in that sense.

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:56.130
Creole is very
specific [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:58.900
target languages if
I'm not mistaken,

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:02.580
so this time [INAUDIBLE]
is much more universal.

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:05.580
It so happens to--

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:12.210
And as for the untangling that
both Rosa and Brandon talked

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:15.750
about, that you wanted
me to connect to,

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:19.560
I think what I found
really interesting is

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:23.520
the way in which we
conceptualize models,

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:31.230
whether it's a primary visual
representation of the model.

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:33.210
I mean, I think in
physics, we have

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:37.560
this added advantage that we
have mathematical equations

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:38.760
that offer--

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:41.790
and laws, these
tight correlations

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:45.160
and cause and effect, which
are very clearly defined.

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:47.250
So what I found
really intriguing is

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:50.550
to hear from two
people in two fields

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:55.260
where causality is not-- for
us, causality is a constraint.

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:59.370
I mean, the cause happens
before the effect,

00:16:59.370 --> 00:17:02.460
and you can actually draw
a line and connect the dots

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:05.040
because you have these
principles, these universal law

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:07.030
of gravitation, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:11.280
So it's very interesting to see
Rosa's work and Brandon's work,

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:15.839
where there aren't necessarily
such clear cut joining

00:17:15.839 --> 00:17:17.700
the dots.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:21.030
In Rosa's case, you
probably impose that by hand

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:23.910
by choosing to compress
the data, decompress

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:28.410
and compress the data, alter
the resolution, and so on.

00:17:31.878 --> 00:17:32.920
Well, thank you for that.

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:36.744
I mean, that-- again,
go ahead, Rosa.

00:17:36.744 --> 00:17:41.620
Yeah, I enjoy this bridge to
what you're talking about,

00:17:41.620 --> 00:17:44.620
and in this case, I would
say it connects mostly

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:48.760
towards the blob that I ended
the video with, which I use

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:50.350
also as a tool, I would say.

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:55.722
It's a tool to not only show how
image making works-- and a part

00:17:55.722 --> 00:17:56.680
of that is compression.

00:17:56.680 --> 00:17:58.270
I wouldn't say that's
the only thing.

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:01.690
Resolution setting has a lot
of stuff involved, whether it's

00:18:01.690 --> 00:18:07.570
the CCD chip or the aperture or
the amount of energy invested

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:09.940
in the machine that
is making the image.

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:15.940
So in that case, this blob is
this metaphoric latent space

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:18.290
for any possible image,
and you cut it away,

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:20.890
and I think there's an
analogy to your research

00:18:20.890 --> 00:18:24.070
also, where you're
talking about affordances.

00:18:24.070 --> 00:18:28.720
I see these affordances
are axes that got the blob,

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:31.120
and they cut away compromises.

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:32.890
So the compromises
are the images

00:18:32.890 --> 00:18:37.712
that are no longer being made
because you set a resolution.

00:18:37.712 --> 00:18:39.170
I think you used
the word for that.

00:18:39.170 --> 00:18:40.870
I'm trying to find
it real quick.

00:18:43.793 --> 00:18:45.460
Do you remember this
word that you used?

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:47.575
I think it was a
very interesting--

00:18:47.575 --> 00:18:49.150
I did use resolution.

00:18:49.150 --> 00:18:54.130
I did use resolution in a
slightly different sense

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:55.750
in terms of--

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:58.010
when I was talking
more about the limits,

00:18:58.010 --> 00:19:00.100
Rosa, which I think
you also come to.

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:01.925
When you're talking
about the blob,

00:19:01.925 --> 00:19:04.300
you are talking about sort of
the limits of understanding

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:06.640
of what is possible.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:08.470
Yeah, I think [INAUDIBLE]--

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:12.990
input assumptions.

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:15.240
That's what I'm
talking about even.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:18.150
That's what you're
saying, and I think

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:20.550
input assumptions are
a very interesting way

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:24.390
to frame-- it's the same idea,
but for me, I use the word

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:25.440
affordance, I guess.

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:37.650
Well, maybe I can
bring Brandon in here.

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:44.810
I'm really interested in this
question that you brought up,

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:49.280
Brandon, about the level at
which abstractions operate

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:54.440
in models, which to use
Rosa's term just now,

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:57.110
involves a set of compromises.

00:19:57.110 --> 00:20:00.320
In order to decide
that a phenomenon is

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:04.580
made of particular levels,
you have to compromise.

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:07.550
So I wonder if you can
speak a little bit more

00:20:07.550 --> 00:20:11.100
about that question
of how you think

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:17.720
through the levels at which
the models you write operate.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:20.350
Maybe I'll just start
asking to start there.

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:21.190
I've got more, but--

00:20:21.190 --> 00:20:21.950
Yeah.

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:23.470
Yeah, yeah, yeah, I think--

00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:25.330
thank you for that question.

00:20:25.330 --> 00:20:27.520
Fabulous interaction already.

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:31.120
Very, very fun to explore
the connections here.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:33.310
I think what's
interesting and fun

00:20:33.310 --> 00:20:36.490
about talking about the
levels of a model question

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:38.890
is that it really does
invoke [INAUDIBLE] expertise

00:20:38.890 --> 00:20:41.740
in the art part of modeling.

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:44.260
That's actually where the
imagination enters it.

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:46.090
That's where
understanding enters it,

00:20:46.090 --> 00:20:49.480
and this is why the notion
of simply having students,

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:53.770
for example, learn just a
suite of mathematical and

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:56.740
computational techniques
is the end all

00:20:56.740 --> 00:20:58.340
be all to being
able to understand,

00:20:58.340 --> 00:21:01.240
for example, what underlies
the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:21:01.240 --> 00:21:04.030
And this is where a lot of
the problems of modeling

00:21:04.030 --> 00:21:07.750
a complex system
comes from, is that we

00:21:07.750 --> 00:21:09.330
have an infinite number--

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:12.850
a lot of different tools we
can use to examine phenomena,

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:16.550
but we aren't clear about
what the question actually is.

00:21:16.550 --> 00:21:19.000
And we have to be clear about
what the question actually

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:23.590
is to build the proper model
to address that question.

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:25.970
So like I talk
about in the talk,

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:28.330
with regards to the
electrons in the calculator,

00:21:28.330 --> 00:21:30.220
let's just take an
epidemic, for example.

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:32.830
I mean, I could
understand everything

00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:36.160
about the way proteins fold.

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:37.690
Let's just say we
completely solve

00:21:37.690 --> 00:21:41.110
that problem, one that's very,
very modern and cutting edge.

00:21:41.110 --> 00:21:43.720
I mean, that could tell you
absolutely nothing about

00:21:43.720 --> 00:21:46.700
whether or not a vaccine
is going to be effective.

00:21:46.700 --> 00:21:49.348
That's a completely different
question at a societal level.

00:21:49.348 --> 00:21:50.890
It's a completely
different question,

00:21:50.890 --> 00:21:52.990
requires a completely different
level of understanding

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:54.760
and requires a
different model in order

00:21:54.760 --> 00:21:56.460
to address that question.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:59.320
I think we can walk that
over into every single one

00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:00.070
of these problems.

00:22:00.070 --> 00:22:03.250
Certainly, in physics,
and it's very humbling

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:04.870
to be on the stage
with a physicist

00:22:04.870 --> 00:22:08.830
because I think a lot of
the modelers in my field

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:12.160
wish they were physics,
the physics envy concept.

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:14.710
I think we've borrowed from
this tradition of being

00:22:14.710 --> 00:22:17.350
able to understand
the world in terms

00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:20.290
of a set of analytical
descriptions,

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:24.430
and what we're finding is
that those are powerful,

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:27.430
but you do require this other
dimension in understanding

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:29.110
and art and insight
in order to be

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:33.410
able to capture a biological
phenomenon in a meaningful way.

00:22:33.410 --> 00:22:36.220
So just to summarize, I mean,
I think that level of question

00:22:36.220 --> 00:22:39.400
is one that is
humbling, but I think

00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:44.920
that is where the creativity,
the domain expertise, and study

00:22:44.920 --> 00:22:46.490
and these types
of things really,

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:49.360
really do separate the good
models from the bad ones,

00:22:49.360 --> 00:22:51.550
and we're seeing this
play out in phenomenon

00:22:51.550 --> 00:22:53.940
like the pandemic.

00:22:53.940 --> 00:22:56.690
Yeah, can I ask you
more about that?

00:22:56.690 --> 00:23:00.890
Since we do have here as an
evolutionary biologist who

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:04.280
thinks a lot about epidemiology
and its various entailments,

00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:08.960
how would you
classify and evaluate

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:15.950
the levels at which the COVID-19
epidemic models are operating?

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:16.933
What are the levels--

00:23:16.933 --> 00:23:17.600
Great question--

00:23:17.600 --> 00:23:19.970
[INAUDIBLE] Go ahead.

00:23:19.970 --> 00:23:24.220
Well, great question, and I
think now, as of April 2021,

00:23:24.220 --> 00:23:26.450
I think all of the
levels of models

00:23:26.450 --> 00:23:29.030
are operating to address
different questions.

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:31.010
But let's go a year ago.

00:23:31.010 --> 00:23:33.890
I think it's fun to think about
this as a chronological thing

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:38.420
because, early on, we were quite
interested in how mutation A

00:23:38.420 --> 00:23:40.850
and mutation B were playing
together on the [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:43.820
protein, and whether
or not that allows--

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:46.700
whether that means the vaccine
will be effective or not.

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:49.347
We wanted to know the basic
shape of the epidemic,

00:23:49.347 --> 00:23:51.680
and of course, you know this
better than anyone, Stefan,

00:23:51.680 --> 00:23:54.380
with regards to the
shape and the wave

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:57.770
analogies and these things
that you've pioneered.

00:23:57.770 --> 00:23:59.510
We wanted to know
the basic shape.

00:23:59.510 --> 00:24:02.060
How bad is this
thing going to be?

00:24:02.060 --> 00:24:03.830
How fast before
we get to A and B?

00:24:03.830 --> 00:24:06.080
How long do we need
to be under lockdown?

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:08.438
Those are macro scale questions.

00:24:08.438 --> 00:24:09.980
In order to understand
that, you need

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:12.050
to some kind of
gross properties,

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:14.120
and the debate about
this [INAUDIBLE] thing,

00:24:14.120 --> 00:24:16.850
which is now common knowledge,
this parameter that we

00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:20.730
use to understand how contagious
a pathogen is, for example.

00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:22.170
It's now common knowledge.

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:26.180
But the idea here is this allows
us to give a general character

00:24:26.180 --> 00:24:28.520
and shape about the
way an epidemic looks,

00:24:28.520 --> 00:24:33.860
and that allowed us to make big
national and social policies.

00:24:33.860 --> 00:24:36.860
That's where the debate really
still is there in some ways,

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:38.630
about what we should do and
when we should open and when we

00:24:38.630 --> 00:24:39.260
shouldn't.

00:24:39.260 --> 00:24:41.060
But that defined
the early window,

00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:44.630
and that's one level at
which models are useful.

00:24:44.630 --> 00:24:46.430
So the early UK
model, for example,

00:24:46.430 --> 00:24:48.380
that talked about a
couple of million dead,

00:24:48.380 --> 00:24:50.930
and these predictions that
were very, very dire if we

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:52.530
don't do anything in a hurry.

00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:55.160
I mean, that's been debated,
but that was at one level.

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:58.320
I think, now, we're at
a much different level,

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:01.587
or rather, our models
include other levels.

00:25:01.587 --> 00:25:03.170
And they are about
things like whether

00:25:03.170 --> 00:25:06.410
or not we can expect the vaccine
to be effective against the UK

00:25:06.410 --> 00:25:09.740
strain or the Brazil
strain or the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:11.930
That's a completely
different question.

00:25:11.930 --> 00:25:16.010
What's interesting and fun to
this point about interglots

00:25:16.010 --> 00:25:17.860
that Priya's present--

00:25:17.860 --> 00:25:20.030
what an amazing term
that I'm certainly

00:25:20.030 --> 00:25:21.810
going to take moving forward.

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:24.020
What a brilliant way
of thinking about it.

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:25.520
It's connecting
these two things.

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:27.380
It's connecting the levels.

00:25:27.380 --> 00:25:29.310
And so models can
allow you to do that.

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:32.780
You can build a model about
the molecular evolution

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:34.680
of the virus at
the protein level,

00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:38.360
and you can ask a question
now about the way that--

00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:40.488
how can we think
about vaccinology

00:25:40.488 --> 00:25:42.530
across a population over
the next several months?

00:25:42.530 --> 00:25:45.280
We can actually build
bridges between these things.

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:46.370
So I find that to be--

00:25:46.370 --> 00:25:48.290
we had to arrive there, though.

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:49.100
It took us time.

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:54.390
I just wanted to make
a very quick point.

00:25:54.390 --> 00:25:57.120
This is just
absolutely fascinating,

00:25:57.120 --> 00:26:02.300
and I think what is
remarkable about this panel

00:26:02.300 --> 00:26:07.880
and the themes are how well
all of the different strands

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:11.480
and types that we all
used to think converge.

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:13.640
And so the COVID
question, for example,

00:26:13.640 --> 00:26:16.490
for me, from what
Brendan has outlined

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:18.680
and Stefan's work
and Rosa's work--

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:20.270
I mean, don't you
guys think this

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:25.430
was one of the absolute
exemplars of the provisionality

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:26.660
of science?

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:28.970
We really don't even
know, as Brendan said,

00:26:28.970 --> 00:26:30.830
what is the right
question to ask?

00:26:30.830 --> 00:26:33.560
What are all the
right questions?

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:37.070
And we've seen all
these various modelers

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:40.130
coming in with their
inherent biases,

00:26:40.130 --> 00:26:43.790
wanting to focus on
one particular question

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:47.150
without realizing that no
one question can be isolated

00:26:47.150 --> 00:26:48.620
in something like the pandemic?

00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:49.580
This is a network.

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:51.650
There are all these
questions that all

00:26:51.650 --> 00:26:55.700
have to be tackled
together, and it's not

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:59.120
like you have, there's
one equation for spread,

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:00.950
that that's all
we needed to get,

00:27:00.950 --> 00:27:02.510
and that would
resolve everything.

00:27:02.510 --> 00:27:05.480
No, this is a very
complex problem,

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:09.290
and I thought this was one
of those classic cases where

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:12.140
you see the process of science
and the provisionality.

00:27:12.140 --> 00:27:15.770
We know as much as we
know at any given point,

00:27:15.770 --> 00:27:18.890
and then we have to, as Brandon
said and as Rosa points out,

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:22.310
we have to, without limit,
keep asking questions

00:27:22.310 --> 00:27:25.070
because we don't even know
what the right questions are

00:27:25.070 --> 00:27:27.830
and which all questions
need to be answered

00:27:27.830 --> 00:27:32.810
to find the solution,
solution being how

00:27:32.810 --> 00:27:34.610
do we survive this as a globe.

00:27:36.843 --> 00:27:37.760
[? TRANSCRIPT BREAK ?]

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:39.470
I think this is so interesting.

00:27:39.470 --> 00:27:42.110
I haven't thought about
the pandemic in such a way,

00:27:42.110 --> 00:27:44.330
but I would say what
you're proposing

00:27:44.330 --> 00:27:49.550
is almost that the pandemic is
a scenario in which scientists

00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:56.000
could almost have a real-life
test scenario in which they can

00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:01.880
communicate, and open up their
knowledge to the normal public,

00:28:01.880 --> 00:28:04.190
which is something
people like me-- artists,

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:05.780
artistic researchers-- do.

00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:08.240
That's my job, basically.

00:28:08.240 --> 00:28:11.165
I do research, but
I don't convey it

00:28:11.165 --> 00:28:12.770
to the scientific
community per se.

00:28:12.770 --> 00:28:16.130
I convey it to a normal
audience that watches art.

00:28:16.130 --> 00:28:17.960
So it needs to be like--

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:23.840
there is a basic necessity
to make it accessible,

00:28:23.840 --> 00:28:25.730
and people wanting to access it.

00:28:25.730 --> 00:28:28.910
And what Brandon is
also saying, we're

00:28:28.910 --> 00:28:31.920
missing kind of a
translational aesthetics here.

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:36.350
And as you're also pointing out
with the Kip Thorne example,

00:28:36.350 --> 00:28:42.350
where the artistic director
of a movie about the shadow

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:44.720
of a black horse only
finds a piece of science

00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:47.867
by showing it through
their [? renders. ?]

00:28:47.867 --> 00:28:50.450
What's really interesting, and
what's been really interesting,

00:28:50.450 --> 00:28:52.790
is that what I've seen
throughout my research

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:58.400
is that there is a very deep
relationality between Hollywood

00:28:58.400 --> 00:29:02.690
aesthetics and aesthetics
of science and programming.

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:07.100
Because programming being
Silicon Valley and Hollywood

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:10.520
being in LA, those are places
that are almost next door.

00:29:10.520 --> 00:29:12.840
And these people actually
come and visit each other.

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:13.820
They conversate.

00:29:13.820 --> 00:29:16.835
Is this study, is this
way of showing this,

00:29:16.835 --> 00:29:18.500
is the proper way to do it?

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:21.680
And then there is a
scientific aesthetic exchange.

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:22.830
This has been happening.

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:28.250
But at this point now,
we're seeing, as you already

00:29:28.250 --> 00:29:31.040
pointed out, that there's people
from all over the disciplines

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:33.680
that all think that their
one particular question is

00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:36.050
the question to answer the
problem of the pandemic.

00:29:36.050 --> 00:29:37.660
But they need to voice this.

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:41.360
And so what we're seeing is that
there is not enough bridges.

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:45.140
There's not enough
[? interglottery ?]

00:29:45.140 --> 00:29:53.270
to voice the problems in
between research fields.

00:29:53.270 --> 00:29:56.900
What I would propose, because
I'm actually quite worried

00:29:56.900 --> 00:29:59.780
about just an interglot,
because to me,

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:02.060
it becomes very
closely connected

00:30:02.060 --> 00:30:04.070
to a newspeak of sciences.

00:30:04.070 --> 00:30:08.720
Because newspeak is
this 1984 reference

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:12.620
to George Orwell, where they're
creating this one language that

00:30:12.620 --> 00:30:14.390
takes out all the
metaphoric speak

00:30:14.390 --> 00:30:20.060
because now we have one word
to capture a particular state.

00:30:20.060 --> 00:30:25.370
This one-on-one idea of
translation, I do hope--

00:30:25.370 --> 00:30:27.530
and I think that's
what you are also for--

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:32.000
is that there is still
this metaphoric analogous

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:34.130
poetic space in the interglot.

00:30:34.130 --> 00:30:36.620
And maybe there is some
space for an extraglot,

00:30:36.620 --> 00:30:37.235
in that sense.

00:30:39.890 --> 00:30:48.080
So I think that one other
interesting thread that I

00:30:48.080 --> 00:30:50.720
see in this mentioned,
one of the reasons

00:30:50.720 --> 00:30:53.000
the pandemic has been
so problematic, aside

00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:59.330
from the impact on our lives,
is the reaction of the public.

00:30:59.330 --> 00:31:03.270
The fact that the science
was evolving in real time,

00:31:03.270 --> 00:31:06.530
our understanding limited by
the questions, as Brandon said,

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:09.320
limited by the level at
which we want to solve.

00:31:09.320 --> 00:31:12.170
And so people are
seeing the uncertainty

00:31:12.170 --> 00:31:15.110
in science, proportionality
and uncertainty.

00:31:15.110 --> 00:31:17.222
Because people were
supposedly baffled,

00:31:17.222 --> 00:31:19.430
when we were saying no masks
today and then tomorrow,

00:31:19.430 --> 00:31:20.587
you're saying masks.

00:31:20.587 --> 00:31:21.170
We don't know.

00:31:21.170 --> 00:31:22.770
All of this is changing.

00:31:22.770 --> 00:31:28.850
And I think this is the peril of
getting into unknown territory,

00:31:28.850 --> 00:31:31.100
moving into unknown territory.

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:37.740
And what are the sort of
metaphorical connections,

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:41.810
and as Brandon said, the levels
and the kinds of connections

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:44.990
that we have to
simultaneously be aware of?

00:31:44.990 --> 00:31:48.410
And to me, it seems
like for the pandemic,

00:31:48.410 --> 00:31:51.890
it might have been
interesting to have started

00:31:51.890 --> 00:31:55.520
off thinking of how to
approach this pandemic

00:31:55.520 --> 00:31:58.940
and modeling it as a crosses
disciplinary adventure

00:31:58.940 --> 00:32:02.360
right from the beginning,
instead of focusing

00:32:02.360 --> 00:32:04.790
on the people who are
modeling contagions,

00:32:04.790 --> 00:32:08.660
providing different models, and
then the public health people

00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:11.940
chiming in as a result,
reacting to the model.

00:32:11.940 --> 00:32:17.780
So I'd be curious to hear all
of you talk about the reactions

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:18.770
to models.

00:32:18.770 --> 00:32:22.760
I mean, why isn't the
modeling more integrative?

00:32:25.580 --> 00:32:27.350
Just for a brief--
just to jump in,

00:32:27.350 --> 00:32:29.120
and I know there's a
lot of COVID fatigue,

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:31.820
and we wouldn't want to
spend the whole time talking

00:32:31.820 --> 00:32:37.340
about that example, but I must
say to connect these points,

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:39.620
I think for all the negatives
and all the things that

00:32:39.620 --> 00:32:41.160
were botched-- and I've
written a lot about them

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:43.470
and I think Priya, you
mentioned a lot about the amount

00:32:43.470 --> 00:32:44.690
the problems we have.

00:32:44.690 --> 00:32:48.470
I think a positive example of
particularly where the arts was

00:32:48.470 --> 00:32:52.490
very central in a
positive response

00:32:52.490 --> 00:32:54.860
was the Flatten the
Curve movement, which

00:32:54.860 --> 00:32:59.990
was a data visualization that
was created by a data artist.

00:32:59.990 --> 00:33:04.230
And flattening the curve became
a social justice rallying cry.

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:06.530
It became an epidemiological
rallying cry.

00:33:06.530 --> 00:33:11.520
And it really defined the
positive part of the response.

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:16.400
And that was not invoked or
created by scientists at all.

00:33:16.400 --> 00:33:19.310
Well, it was somebody who
was a data visualization

00:33:19.310 --> 00:33:22.077
person that ended up driving
this public health campaign.

00:33:22.077 --> 00:33:23.660
So I think to the
point you just made,

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:25.940
Priya, that type
of good example--

00:33:25.940 --> 00:33:27.350
and that is a model.

00:33:27.350 --> 00:33:31.928
There's no numbers in it,
but it is a model in the ways

00:33:31.928 --> 00:33:32.720
that we talk about.

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:34.262
And it is a model,
certainly, the way

00:33:34.262 --> 00:33:36.710
that Rosa has mentioned with
regards to how the arts can

00:33:36.710 --> 00:33:38.810
drive and kind of
reframe how we can

00:33:38.810 --> 00:33:42.630
think about complex problems.

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:47.200
I would expand
that to include it

00:33:47.200 --> 00:33:50.110
as an interglot, that
kind of synthesis.

00:33:52.910 --> 00:33:54.325
Yeah, I mean,
thanks for bringing

00:33:54.325 --> 00:33:56.450
that up, Brandon, because
I was going to think of--

00:33:56.450 --> 00:34:01.220
precisely following Rosa--
think about the politics

00:34:01.220 --> 00:34:04.430
and the possible pitfalls
of aesthetic productions

00:34:04.430 --> 00:34:07.550
of scientific knowledge
in visualization.

00:34:07.550 --> 00:34:09.770
The curve has done a
lot of really good work,

00:34:09.770 --> 00:34:12.110
but it also obscures things.

00:34:12.110 --> 00:34:14.900
I mean, it obscures the
multiplicity of curves.

00:34:14.900 --> 00:34:17.659
It did for a while,
I think for many,

00:34:17.659 --> 00:34:20.690
obscure the radically
different experiences

00:34:20.690 --> 00:34:23.120
different communities
had, where one

00:34:23.120 --> 00:34:26.120
had to realize there are
many, many curves happening.

00:34:26.120 --> 00:34:29.659
And some of the
curves are revelatory

00:34:29.659 --> 00:34:32.060
of things like
structural racism,

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:34.760
underserved public
health infrastructure,

00:34:34.760 --> 00:34:38.000
things like that.

00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:39.889
But I think that's
super interesting

00:34:39.889 --> 00:34:44.330
to think about that piece
of scientific visualization

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:48.350
as producing an aesthetics
that does and does not

00:34:48.350 --> 00:34:51.530
surface particular kinds
of matters of concern.

00:34:51.530 --> 00:34:54.184
I want to get back to
aesthetics and ask Rosa

00:34:54.184 --> 00:35:02.060
a question about aesthetics.

00:35:02.060 --> 00:35:06.380
And I want to ask you
what your experience was

00:35:06.380 --> 00:35:10.770
of watching the talks by Priya
and Brandon in terms of that.

00:35:10.770 --> 00:35:14.420
What kinds of aesthetic
trends did you

00:35:14.420 --> 00:35:18.560
see kind of coursing through the
way they presented their talks?

00:35:18.560 --> 00:35:20.900
Maybe you have some
kind of glitch radar.

00:35:20.900 --> 00:35:26.270
What kinds of glitches did
you see that might possibly

00:35:26.270 --> 00:35:30.320
leap into their work
unannounced or things

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:33.130
like that, if that makes sense.

00:35:33.130 --> 00:35:35.380
Honestly, I didn't
see any glitches.

00:35:35.380 --> 00:35:40.600
Also, I have to say I
appreciate glitches.

00:35:40.600 --> 00:35:44.350
I think we are diluting the
word "glitch" a little bit here.

00:35:44.350 --> 00:35:48.520
A glitch would be an unexpected
break from an expected

00:35:48.520 --> 00:35:51.490
flow or a functional flow.

00:35:51.490 --> 00:35:53.590
Of course, in
popular culture, this

00:35:53.590 --> 00:35:55.630
has become something
more stylized

00:35:55.630 --> 00:35:58.300
and it's been taken out of
the technological definition.

00:35:58.300 --> 00:36:01.090
And now it's become
like a language,

00:36:01.090 --> 00:36:03.040
a visual language of its own.

00:36:03.040 --> 00:36:07.090
When I looked at Brandon
simply as a presentation,

00:36:07.090 --> 00:36:09.820
I don't think there
was a lot of glitch

00:36:09.820 --> 00:36:12.010
there whatsoever, not
from the stylized form

00:36:12.010 --> 00:36:13.990
and definitely
not what I can say

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:16.120
for a scientific point of view.

00:36:16.120 --> 00:36:17.080
You guys are perfect.

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:25.220
I mean, maybe another term to
put in here is "standards."

00:36:25.220 --> 00:36:27.310
I mean, what kinds
of standards did you

00:36:27.310 --> 00:36:30.790
see animating the
way the work had

00:36:30.790 --> 00:36:34.120
to be kind of held together?

00:36:34.120 --> 00:36:39.220
Well, what I like about
Priya's presentation,

00:36:39.220 --> 00:36:43.450
but maybe that says more about
me than about scientific,

00:36:43.450 --> 00:36:44.890
but maybe not--

00:36:44.890 --> 00:36:47.860
when I was going through
her slides again,

00:36:47.860 --> 00:36:49.660
I recognized a lot
of the images that I

00:36:49.660 --> 00:36:51.130
use in my own presentations.

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:54.400
There was one slide that you
have three different images.

00:36:54.400 --> 00:36:58.930
One was from the Big Bang
and the time after it.

00:36:58.930 --> 00:37:05.020
There was the shadow of a
black hole, and another image.

00:37:05.020 --> 00:37:07.780
And in my last
presentations, I've

00:37:07.780 --> 00:37:12.160
been using those particular
images point blank.

00:37:12.160 --> 00:37:15.595
So what I'm starting
to see is that there

00:37:15.595 --> 00:37:19.720
are these tropes or images
that are maybe overused.

00:37:19.720 --> 00:37:24.550
They become not just
an illustration,

00:37:24.550 --> 00:37:26.230
they become the illustration.

00:37:26.230 --> 00:37:29.740
And that's actually
dangerous, because then we

00:37:29.740 --> 00:37:32.950
consider a black hole or
the shadow of a black hole,

00:37:32.950 --> 00:37:35.870
in this case, to always
have these particular

00:37:35.870 --> 00:37:39.760
[? floopsies ?] of
energy or whatever it is.

00:37:39.760 --> 00:37:44.500
And I think that is dangerous
because maybe black holes are

00:37:44.500 --> 00:37:47.860
not at all this round,
and also because these

00:37:47.860 --> 00:37:50.140
are simulated images in
the sense that they have

00:37:50.140 --> 00:37:52.220
been given color or whatever.

00:37:52.220 --> 00:38:00.730
So for the non-educated
scientific interested person,

00:38:00.730 --> 00:38:08.020
they might actually open up just
a standardized aesthetic that

00:38:08.020 --> 00:38:11.740
makes them misunderstand that
these things are much more

00:38:11.740 --> 00:38:14.560
wide in variety, and that
a lot of these things

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:18.910
can be colored differently,
be understood differently--

00:38:18.910 --> 00:38:20.650
basically that what
you're looking at

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:23.590
are like energy
waves or sound waves

00:38:23.590 --> 00:38:26.260
rather than a color, a shadow.

00:38:26.260 --> 00:38:30.058
I don't know, whatever.

00:38:30.058 --> 00:38:30.600
So that's a--

00:38:30.600 --> 00:38:33.630
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:38:33.630 --> 00:38:36.870
--very important point
you make, Rosa, and Stefan

00:38:36.870 --> 00:38:37.890
for guiding her to that.

00:38:37.890 --> 00:38:40.110
I think that these
images are meant

00:38:40.110 --> 00:38:42.280
to structure our imagination.

00:38:42.280 --> 00:38:45.990
But there is a fear they
will limit our imagination.

00:38:45.990 --> 00:38:48.750
And we have to
watch out for that.

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:52.290
And I have something
to add to that, maybe.

00:38:52.290 --> 00:38:56.160
When I was at CERN where I did
my Collider at CERN residency,

00:38:56.160 --> 00:39:00.060
I was really looking
for imagery aesthetics

00:39:00.060 --> 00:39:03.870
of particle physics, research
into the smallest, tiniest

00:39:03.870 --> 00:39:04.500
bits.

00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:06.630
And what I found was
that there was actually

00:39:06.630 --> 00:39:09.690
very little artistic
interpretation of those places.

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:15.690
All you see are just the events
in a collision diagram or just

00:39:15.690 --> 00:39:16.710
numbers.

00:39:16.710 --> 00:39:19.170
And I was wondering
a lot of the times,

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:22.110
why is there so little
artistic interpretation

00:39:22.110 --> 00:39:23.610
in a place like CERN?

00:39:23.610 --> 00:39:28.470
And my conclusion was finally
that we don't actually

00:39:28.470 --> 00:39:31.080
have a lot of movies taking
place at the particle physics

00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:31.990
level.

00:39:31.990 --> 00:39:39.690
So there is not that influx of
money, of artistic directors,

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:47.070
of research into how to open up
this space to another audience

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:51.330
which has, again, both positive
and negative consequences.

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:54.930
But so when I was
there, actually, I

00:39:54.930 --> 00:39:57.570
believe it was
also Kip Thorne who

00:39:57.570 --> 00:39:59.250
came to visit
because he was going

00:39:59.250 --> 00:40:03.330
to make a movie about tiny
superheroes or something.

00:40:03.330 --> 00:40:04.080
I don't know.

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:06.060
But that was really
funny to me, because I

00:40:06.060 --> 00:40:09.090
think it's one of the first
times they had a Hollywood

00:40:09.090 --> 00:40:13.020
director going to do research
on the tiny stuff, which

00:40:13.020 --> 00:40:16.950
is something that there's not
a lot of imagination about,

00:40:16.950 --> 00:40:20.290
that we all have to imagine
by ourselves somehow.

00:40:20.290 --> 00:40:24.360
But I think in astronomy, we
have the advantage, thankfully,

00:40:24.360 --> 00:40:26.740
that the sky
belongs to everyone.

00:40:26.740 --> 00:40:29.250
And we all have experience.

00:40:29.250 --> 00:40:32.220
We don't have to do much,
just existing, looking up,

00:40:32.220 --> 00:40:36.330
being upright as Homo
sapiens, we've had access.

00:40:36.330 --> 00:40:39.780
So I mean there's a connection
that anybody and everybody can

00:40:39.780 --> 00:40:42.810
make to our visual images.

00:40:42.810 --> 00:40:48.300
And there isn't the same kind of
visceral connection and access.

00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:51.570
A child growing up in Kenya
looking up at the night

00:40:51.570 --> 00:40:54.240
will see a better sky, actually,
than we do in New Haven,

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:54.870
for instance.

00:40:54.870 --> 00:40:58.020
But you don't have the
same with particle physics.

00:40:58.020 --> 00:41:01.590
There's a lot of layers
of interpretation,

00:41:01.590 --> 00:41:07.260
computing, models, many layers
of many unfolded and folded

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:13.110
models that lie between us and
that representation of data

00:41:13.110 --> 00:41:17.200
from particle accelerators.

00:41:17.200 --> 00:41:19.240
And also, to use
Rosa's terms again,

00:41:19.240 --> 00:41:22.810
there are compromises
built into the kinds

00:41:22.810 --> 00:41:26.110
of theoretical commitments that
you want to make in the model.

00:41:29.080 --> 00:41:32.530
I want to make sure we get
some time to get questions

00:41:32.530 --> 00:41:33.950
from our audience.

00:41:33.950 --> 00:41:37.350
And I have a couple here.

00:41:37.350 --> 00:41:40.510
Maybe I'll start
with one to Brandon.

00:41:40.510 --> 00:41:42.780
We have a question.

00:41:42.780 --> 00:41:43.730
I'll read it.

00:41:43.730 --> 00:41:47.450
It says, "You shared that
abstractions and metaphors are

00:41:47.450 --> 00:41:51.170
how incomplete models
could be generative.

00:41:51.170 --> 00:41:53.510
In your observations,
would these poetics

00:41:53.510 --> 00:41:56.287
be challenging for
scientists to grasp?

00:41:56.287 --> 00:41:58.370
What are your recommendations
for young scientists

00:41:58.370 --> 00:42:02.390
to enhance competence in using
abstractions and metaphors

00:42:02.390 --> 00:42:05.640
in modeling?"

00:42:05.640 --> 00:42:06.640
That's a great question.

00:42:10.210 --> 00:42:15.550
I think the challenge here is
the way that we teach science

00:42:15.550 --> 00:42:18.610
and how that is incompatible
with a lot of the ideas

00:42:18.610 --> 00:42:22.270
that I talked about with
regards to how abstractions

00:42:22.270 --> 00:42:24.170
can be generative.

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:26.860
We teach people kind
of the analytical tools

00:42:26.860 --> 00:42:31.150
to be able to understand,
in physics class,

00:42:31.150 --> 00:42:34.390
the way a pendulum is swinging
or something of that nature,

00:42:34.390 --> 00:42:36.370
which is a really
important thing

00:42:36.370 --> 00:42:37.870
to understand because
those are kind

00:42:37.870 --> 00:42:40.360
of fundamental forces
of the universe.

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:43.930
The problem is that
everything that is interesting

00:42:43.930 --> 00:42:48.130
will not be neatly described
by those types of equations

00:42:48.130 --> 00:42:48.760
that way.

00:42:48.760 --> 00:42:51.010
And a great example,
one that I think

00:42:51.010 --> 00:42:53.710
of when I connect
with Rosa's work,

00:42:53.710 --> 00:42:57.640
is the whole problem
of modern genetics,

00:42:57.640 --> 00:43:01.240
is one where we have
a complicated mess

00:43:01.240 --> 00:43:04.960
of genetic information
interacting with environments

00:43:04.960 --> 00:43:09.100
in all these essentially
infinitely complicated ways

00:43:09.100 --> 00:43:12.880
to create me standing
here at what am I,

00:43:12.880 --> 00:43:15.542
let's just round up to 6 foot 1.

00:43:15.542 --> 00:43:21.160
And the idea that is composed
of all of this information

00:43:21.160 --> 00:43:23.570
interacting right in this way.

00:43:23.570 --> 00:43:25.720
And I think we're
limited in that way

00:43:25.720 --> 00:43:27.880
by the manner in
which we have built

00:43:27.880 --> 00:43:31.270
scientific knowledge,
which is 1 plus 1 equals 2,

00:43:31.270 --> 00:43:33.880
and everything is very
linear and structure,

00:43:33.880 --> 00:43:35.800
when in fact, it's this blob.

00:43:35.800 --> 00:43:37.270
It's this tangled bank.

00:43:37.270 --> 00:43:39.460
It's all of these
complicated things that

00:43:39.460 --> 00:43:41.360
are interacting in one way.

00:43:41.360 --> 00:43:44.320
So what I would say is, so how
do you actually address that?

00:43:44.320 --> 00:43:47.410
It's that we really need--

00:43:47.410 --> 00:43:50.392
I think the easy answer
is liberal arts education

00:43:50.392 --> 00:43:51.350
or something like that.

00:43:51.350 --> 00:43:53.990
But I think much more
nuanced than that,

00:43:53.990 --> 00:43:57.033
the best models do
not require numbers.

00:43:57.033 --> 00:43:58.450
And this is what
I say-- it starts

00:43:58.450 --> 00:44:01.455
conceptual and artful first.

00:44:01.455 --> 00:44:02.830
It doesn't matter
how complicated

00:44:02.830 --> 00:44:05.930
the problem is, whether or
not it is Schrodinger's cat,

00:44:05.930 --> 00:44:07.840
and I don't know how
Priya feels about that.

00:44:07.840 --> 00:44:09.382
So maybe you don't
like that analogy.

00:44:09.382 --> 00:44:12.460
But the point is, it's has
been useful for people to think

00:44:12.460 --> 00:44:15.407
about a complicated problem,
whether it's the Punnett

00:44:15.407 --> 00:44:17.740
Square, which is analogy,
whether it's the [? central ?]

00:44:17.740 --> 00:44:19.115
[? dogma, ?] which
is an analogy,

00:44:19.115 --> 00:44:22.060
whether it's another
one that Priya,

00:44:22.060 --> 00:44:26.290
I want to hear you talk about,
the the Michelson-Morley,

00:44:26.290 --> 00:44:27.310
whatever it is.

00:44:27.310 --> 00:44:31.420
I think we really need to teach
abstract reasoning independent

00:44:31.420 --> 00:44:34.720
of numbers or even scientific
principles at first,

00:44:34.720 --> 00:44:38.800
and then use that to build
scientific knowledge on top of.

00:44:38.800 --> 00:44:41.660
I practice this in
my research program.

00:44:41.660 --> 00:44:47.200
And I think this is kind
of useful for young people.

00:44:47.200 --> 00:44:48.600
Well, thank you
so much for that.

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:50.790
That really connects
well to a question

00:44:50.790 --> 00:44:53.760
that's come in for
Rosa, precisely about

00:44:53.760 --> 00:44:55.650
non-numerical representation.

00:44:55.650 --> 00:44:57.780
The question is
about narratives.

00:44:57.780 --> 00:45:02.940
So this is a question from
one of our viewers to Rosa,

00:45:02.940 --> 00:45:07.450
and it reads, "Narratives
seem to be prominent

00:45:07.450 --> 00:45:08.920
in your practice.

00:45:08.920 --> 00:45:11.230
Could you further
elaborate how narratives

00:45:11.230 --> 00:45:14.740
have helped enrich your
artistic research, especially

00:45:14.740 --> 00:45:17.080
in exploring your subject
matters that are often

00:45:17.080 --> 00:45:22.030
quite abstract, resolution,
and data, or even nonexistent.

00:45:22.030 --> 00:45:25.360
This is your work about
impossible images.

00:45:25.360 --> 00:45:27.700
How would you describe the
role of narratives for you

00:45:27.700 --> 00:45:30.580
in formulating,
challenging, expanding

00:45:30.580 --> 00:45:34.920
computationalist and
representationalist models?

00:45:34.920 --> 00:45:38.490
Yeah, I have a twofold
answer to that and one part

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:39.850
I have actually already covered.

00:45:39.850 --> 00:45:44.040
But when I was talking
to scientists at CERN,

00:45:44.040 --> 00:45:47.370
one of the scientists told me
that to her, actually, science

00:45:47.370 --> 00:45:50.100
is the challenge of
finding things out

00:45:50.100 --> 00:45:51.810
and the pleasure
of understanding.

00:45:51.810 --> 00:45:54.450
But then, of course,
as I am an artist

00:45:54.450 --> 00:45:58.080
and I'm not writing a diary or
just doing research in my head

00:45:58.080 --> 00:46:00.810
until I make a paper,
I want to put it

00:46:00.810 --> 00:46:06.940
into legible or understandable
information to my audience.

00:46:06.940 --> 00:46:08.770
But then there was
the second part

00:46:08.770 --> 00:46:10.810
is where, when I was
visiting-- because that's

00:46:10.810 --> 00:46:13.180
the beauty of the CERN
Award that I received

00:46:13.180 --> 00:46:15.820
is that I didn't just
get to be at CERN

00:46:15.820 --> 00:46:18.890
and talk to all the scientists
there, which was amazing.

00:46:18.890 --> 00:46:21.580
I talked to 40
scientists, and I realized

00:46:21.580 --> 00:46:24.670
that there are many types
of particle physicists.

00:46:24.670 --> 00:46:26.650
There is not just one.

00:46:26.650 --> 00:46:29.620
Every particle
physicist by themselves

00:46:29.620 --> 00:46:33.550
already use another language
to talk about the same things.

00:46:33.550 --> 00:46:35.890
And that depends if
they're philosophical

00:46:35.890 --> 00:46:38.320
or more mathematically geared.

00:46:38.320 --> 00:46:42.430
But to me as a newbie, as
somebody that is definitely not

00:46:42.430 --> 00:46:45.940
of that realm, it became really
hard to follow their language

00:46:45.940 --> 00:46:49.930
and to learn to probe what to
be able to expect from them.

00:46:49.930 --> 00:46:52.732
And then it turns out, OK,
they have not one or four

00:46:52.732 --> 00:46:54.190
big experiments,
but actually, they

00:46:54.190 --> 00:46:56.080
have hundreds of experiments.

00:46:56.080 --> 00:46:59.230
And all these experiments,
they perceive stuff

00:46:59.230 --> 00:47:01.150
through technology.

00:47:01.150 --> 00:47:01.750
Cool.

00:47:01.750 --> 00:47:04.060
How do they perceive stuff?

00:47:04.060 --> 00:47:06.400
They watch something,
whether it's

00:47:06.400 --> 00:47:09.640
a wave, whether it's light--
or whether it's a light wave

00:47:09.640 --> 00:47:12.310
or whether it's a sound or
whether it's an energy thing.

00:47:12.310 --> 00:47:12.970
I don't know.

00:47:12.970 --> 00:47:15.320
They watch all this stuff
going through something,

00:47:15.320 --> 00:47:19.480
whether it's a magnet or
a lens, an optical lens.

00:47:19.480 --> 00:47:20.890
They watch it through something.

00:47:20.890 --> 00:47:24.430
They probe it through something
and then something comes out.

00:47:24.430 --> 00:47:27.190
But some of these people, they
try to dumb it down for me

00:47:27.190 --> 00:47:28.670
and try to make
it understandable.

00:47:28.670 --> 00:47:31.458
And they talk about it
as photographic machines.

00:47:31.458 --> 00:47:33.250
And then when I come
to the next scientist,

00:47:33.250 --> 00:47:39.780
they get very upset if I say,
oh, so this part of the camera

00:47:39.780 --> 00:47:40.950
is the lens.

00:47:40.950 --> 00:47:43.800
They're, like, "This
is not a camera."

00:47:43.800 --> 00:47:48.030
So what I realized is that
not just every scientist

00:47:48.030 --> 00:47:49.710
has their own language.

00:47:49.710 --> 00:47:56.430
These machines, they also
are generally called--

00:47:56.430 --> 00:47:58.170
I don't know how to
say it-- referenced

00:47:58.170 --> 00:47:59.500
to by different names.

00:47:59.500 --> 00:48:02.010
And what I really
enjoyed by both Priya

00:48:02.010 --> 00:48:06.570
and Brandon is that Priya,
you used "messengers."

00:48:06.570 --> 00:48:08.820
And I would say
those messengers,

00:48:08.820 --> 00:48:13.530
they go through a window that's
like the probing mechanism,

00:48:13.530 --> 00:48:18.630
whereas in Brandon's talk,
you use the word "actors"

00:48:18.630 --> 00:48:19.980
and "operators."

00:48:19.980 --> 00:48:25.140
I'm not entirely sure if I can
conflate your two researches

00:48:25.140 --> 00:48:27.390
through that, but
in my imagination,

00:48:27.390 --> 00:48:30.240
if we have a latent image
space, or just a latent

00:48:30.240 --> 00:48:34.220
image, or latent
space of perception,

00:48:34.220 --> 00:48:39.200
or whatever it is, then
there are at least these two.

00:48:39.200 --> 00:48:41.580
But now with machine learning,
it gets more complicated,

00:48:41.580 --> 00:48:41.930
of course.

00:48:41.930 --> 00:48:43.370
And that's very
interesting to me

00:48:43.370 --> 00:48:45.620
but I don't know how
to expound on that yet.

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:50.540
But you get this window
messenger kind of situation

00:48:50.540 --> 00:48:54.350
in every one of these
scientific spaces.

00:48:54.350 --> 00:48:57.080
And that refers, again,
to your interglots, Priya,

00:48:57.080 --> 00:48:59.285
but also maybe to Brandon.

00:49:02.090 --> 00:49:07.540
I don't know if that's a good
answer to you, but that's--

00:49:07.540 --> 00:49:12.860
It makes me wonder about
visual interglots, maybe.

00:49:12.860 --> 00:49:15.900
One of the people
asking a question to us

00:49:15.900 --> 00:49:17.900
revisits something we've
discussed a little bit,

00:49:17.900 --> 00:49:20.240
but I think is worth
more discussion.

00:49:20.240 --> 00:49:22.490
For Priya, they write,
"You've described

00:49:22.490 --> 00:49:24.080
the profound potential
of simulations

00:49:24.080 --> 00:49:25.250
and cosmological studies.

00:49:25.250 --> 00:49:28.310
The simulated images you showed
are indeed very stunning.

00:49:28.310 --> 00:49:32.600
Do visual parameters
in your simulation

00:49:32.600 --> 00:49:36.440
serve both functionally
and aesthetically?

00:49:36.440 --> 00:49:38.810
How do you decide on the
determination and altering

00:49:38.810 --> 00:49:41.150
of visual elements
in your simulations,

00:49:41.150 --> 00:49:44.000
like colors, scales,
textures, even

00:49:44.000 --> 00:49:45.830
visual effects and animation?

00:49:45.830 --> 00:49:50.530
And how do they affect
the modeling results?"

00:49:50.530 --> 00:49:53.390
I'm thinking, too,
of Rosa's talk

00:49:53.390 --> 00:49:59.540
pointing us to things like
the latent histories of race

00:49:59.540 --> 00:50:02.420
in color photography,
which tells

00:50:02.420 --> 00:50:07.220
on the very chemistry of
the way that film operated

00:50:07.220 --> 00:50:08.670
in the mid 20th century.

00:50:08.670 --> 00:50:11.690
So what about the visuals
that you give us, the colors

00:50:11.690 --> 00:50:15.100
of the black holes, et cetera?

00:50:15.100 --> 00:50:19.090
Right, so that's
a great question.

00:50:19.090 --> 00:50:22.300
And I think that there
are choices that are made,

00:50:22.300 --> 00:50:26.510
that are very specific
choices that are made.

00:50:26.510 --> 00:50:31.120
And so there is intentionality
behind those choices.

00:50:31.120 --> 00:50:36.010
And often, if they
are optimized,

00:50:36.010 --> 00:50:39.850
as I mentioned, for
familiarity, because these

00:50:39.850 --> 00:50:45.250
are very esoteric,
abstract kinds of objects

00:50:45.250 --> 00:50:47.680
that you can't quite
reach out and touch.

00:50:47.680 --> 00:50:51.940
And so there's a way in
which part of the exercise

00:50:51.940 --> 00:50:55.540
is to make it intelligible
and accessible,

00:50:55.540 --> 00:50:59.950
that is, to a visual palette
that anyone and everyone can

00:50:59.950 --> 00:51:00.970
kind of appreciate.

00:51:00.970 --> 00:51:04.030
So the fundamental
colors of heat--

00:51:04.030 --> 00:51:07.000
the fact that it's white hot,
it's red hot, and yellow.

00:51:07.000 --> 00:51:09.040
So that sort of
black-body spectrum

00:51:09.040 --> 00:51:11.980
sort of informs
many of the choices.

00:51:11.980 --> 00:51:15.160
And some of the
visualization is also

00:51:15.160 --> 00:51:18.430
scientifically driven in
terms of the phenomenon

00:51:18.430 --> 00:51:22.340
that I want to draw your
eye to and attention to.

00:51:22.340 --> 00:51:25.990
So what do you choose
to show as dynamic?

00:51:25.990 --> 00:51:27.410
Because one of the
problems in all

00:51:27.410 --> 00:51:32.440
these simulations is the number
of enmeshed, embedded time

00:51:32.440 --> 00:51:35.980
scales and spatial scales
that are all kind of lumped

00:51:35.980 --> 00:51:37.030
together.

00:51:37.030 --> 00:51:39.580
And in the universe,
when you go out at night

00:51:39.580 --> 00:51:42.640
and you see a little,
wispy galaxy [? nearby, ?]

00:51:42.640 --> 00:51:45.250
you're seeing a two-dimensional
projection of it.

00:51:45.250 --> 00:51:47.410
And the advantage
of a simulation

00:51:47.410 --> 00:51:51.610
is that we can give you the
experience of dancing around

00:51:51.610 --> 00:51:55.540
it and seeing it in 3D,
which you never, ever see.

00:51:55.540 --> 00:51:56.920
So it's a reconstruction.

00:51:56.920 --> 00:52:01.828
So it's a simulation in
sort of the dictionary word.

00:52:01.828 --> 00:52:03.870
If you go and look up the
word of a "simulation,"

00:52:03.870 --> 00:52:07.120
there's something about
fakeness, make-believe,

00:52:07.120 --> 00:52:08.360
that is part of that.

00:52:08.360 --> 00:52:11.140
So there's that little
bit of that element.

00:52:11.140 --> 00:52:13.540
But I wanted to come back
to this larger question

00:52:13.540 --> 00:52:15.790
that everyone has
addressed so beautifully,

00:52:15.790 --> 00:52:18.430
Stefan, which you
pointed out, which

00:52:18.430 --> 00:52:22.780
is I think we need a sort
of a new kind of lexicon.

00:52:22.780 --> 00:52:27.880
And my personal obsessions
are with mapping.

00:52:27.880 --> 00:52:31.330
And so I think we don't, as
Brandon said and Rosa said,

00:52:31.330 --> 00:52:32.650
we can have a new--

00:52:32.650 --> 00:52:35.050
we can come up with
new kinds of alphabet.

00:52:35.050 --> 00:52:37.900
I mean, it doesn't
have to be mathematics.

00:52:37.900 --> 00:52:44.170
It doesn't have to be pre
models that already exist.

00:52:44.170 --> 00:52:48.880
We can really think of
new kind of ways of--

00:52:48.880 --> 00:52:53.110
it seems if you look at any
problem now, it's not so much--

00:52:53.110 --> 00:52:55.280
the problems are no longer--

00:52:55.280 --> 00:52:57.490
you can't force them
into linear bits.

00:52:57.490 --> 00:53:00.200
It's all about networks
and interconnections.

00:53:00.200 --> 00:53:02.080
So I think we need a
new lexicon that'll

00:53:02.080 --> 00:53:04.540
allow you to map connections.

00:53:04.540 --> 00:53:05.620
I don't know what it is.

00:53:05.620 --> 00:53:08.890
[INAUDIBLE] people
like Rosa to help us,

00:53:08.890 --> 00:53:11.860
help people like Brandon
and me to figure out

00:53:11.860 --> 00:53:19.486
what might be optimal ways
of getting people to think,

00:53:19.486 --> 00:53:22.420
both pedagogically
and otherwise.

00:53:22.420 --> 00:53:25.430
What might be new ways of--

00:53:25.430 --> 00:53:30.490
visual clearly, because
of the universal appeal

00:53:30.490 --> 00:53:33.760
of visual elements.

00:53:33.760 --> 00:53:36.547
Although I also would
argue colorblindness.

00:53:36.547 --> 00:53:39.130
I also think of colorblindness
or think of the different kinds

00:53:39.130 --> 00:53:43.960
of [INAUDIBLE],, or the fact
that luminosity and saturation

00:53:43.960 --> 00:53:47.590
don't track the same in black
and white versus in color.

00:53:47.590 --> 00:53:49.900
And so all these
interesting confusions

00:53:49.900 --> 00:53:54.370
can happen when you move from
one coloring regime to another.

00:53:54.370 --> 00:53:56.980
But I'll step back,
and Brandon, you were--

00:53:56.980 --> 00:53:59.740
And the whole sort of inherent--

00:53:59.740 --> 00:54:02.380
the whole idea of
in science, a lot

00:54:02.380 --> 00:54:05.470
of these iconic methodologies--

00:54:05.470 --> 00:54:08.260
there have been a very
small set of people

00:54:08.260 --> 00:54:11.230
who have been able to do
science, who have got to define

00:54:11.230 --> 00:54:12.760
science, who have
gotten to define

00:54:12.760 --> 00:54:14.770
the symbolic value of science.

00:54:14.770 --> 00:54:18.430
And the question is in
converging to universals,

00:54:18.430 --> 00:54:21.850
there's always a danger
that we are drowning

00:54:21.850 --> 00:54:27.220
out the multiplicity
and the creative energy

00:54:27.220 --> 00:54:30.970
that different kinds of
individuals, different kinds

00:54:30.970 --> 00:54:32.390
of systems bring.

00:54:32.390 --> 00:54:35.020
So I think that is the
challenge of our times.

00:54:35.020 --> 00:54:41.470
How do we actually move away
from the restricted visual

00:54:41.470 --> 00:54:44.090
symbolic languages?

00:54:44.090 --> 00:54:45.662
I know that Brandon
wants to add--

00:54:45.662 --> 00:54:46.495
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:54:46.495 --> 00:54:48.370
--anthropologist.

00:54:48.370 --> 00:54:51.820
But I just want to say
maybe what you're both

00:54:51.820 --> 00:54:54.610
getting at is that
most of our audiences

00:54:54.610 --> 00:54:57.880
have become too lazy
because they're so

00:54:57.880 --> 00:55:01.210
used to established
vernaculars and established

00:55:01.210 --> 00:55:03.590
imagery for particular things.

00:55:03.590 --> 00:55:06.790
So maybe what we need is
more dynamic interpretations.

00:55:06.790 --> 00:55:11.110
And I'm also thinking about
the materiality, so not just

00:55:11.110 --> 00:55:14.940
referencing materials,
but also using materiality

00:55:14.940 --> 00:55:21.630
as a signifying
strategy, so to create

00:55:21.630 --> 00:55:26.190
multilayered folkloric
vernaculars to translate

00:55:26.190 --> 00:55:28.905
scientific imagery
that is not static.

00:55:32.177 --> 00:55:33.260
That's absolutely spot on.

00:55:33.260 --> 00:55:36.750
Maybe like an informed
[? material ?]

00:55:36.750 --> 00:55:39.327
vernacular, I would say.

00:55:39.327 --> 00:55:40.160
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:55:40.160 --> 00:55:42.330
--two things come to mind.

00:55:42.330 --> 00:55:44.400
Yeah, this is
really what made me

00:55:44.400 --> 00:55:48.810
converge on this
obsessively on interglot.

00:55:48.810 --> 00:55:51.150
There's something
there, that I feel

00:55:51.150 --> 00:55:55.170
that it has so many
dimensions in which we

00:55:55.170 --> 00:56:00.180
could mine that metaphor.

00:56:00.180 --> 00:56:01.160
Yeah, do you know--

00:56:01.160 --> 00:56:02.290
My dream--

00:56:02.290 --> 00:56:03.230
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:56:03.230 --> 00:56:05.112
--statement of
the forked tongue?

00:56:05.112 --> 00:56:07.070
I just want to put that
in there because that's

00:56:07.070 --> 00:56:09.590
such a nice word for that.

00:56:09.590 --> 00:56:13.010
While you have like
one-layered imagery,

00:56:13.010 --> 00:56:15.490
you could fork it all
the time, forked tongues.

00:56:18.600 --> 00:56:22.440
My dream for the way that I want
to go about this problem just

00:56:22.440 --> 00:56:24.960
personally is--

00:56:24.960 --> 00:56:26.310
this is the dream--

00:56:26.310 --> 00:56:28.620
for every single
scientific discovery,

00:56:28.620 --> 00:56:31.590
I could tell a story
about it, put it

00:56:31.590 --> 00:56:32.910
into kind of a story form.

00:56:32.910 --> 00:56:38.130
Because that is a non-visual
but very universal, kind

00:56:38.130 --> 00:56:40.890
of folkloric, I heard.

00:56:40.890 --> 00:56:43.710
I would be able to also
create a visual piece of art

00:56:43.710 --> 00:56:45.000
around it, which is great.

00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:46.980
And I'll also be able
to gamify it somehow.

00:56:46.980 --> 00:56:48.313
That's one we didn't talk about.

00:56:48.313 --> 00:56:51.930
It's another-- that's
another largely universal.

00:56:51.930 --> 00:56:52.740
That's debatable.

00:56:52.740 --> 00:56:54.485
I'll ask the
anthropologist in the room

00:56:54.485 --> 00:56:55.860
whether or not
[? gamification ?]

00:56:55.860 --> 00:56:57.330
is truly universal.

00:56:57.330 --> 00:56:59.530
But I think there are
certainly arguments of that.

00:56:59.530 --> 00:57:01.950
And so what I'm saying is,
I wonder if this interglot,

00:57:01.950 --> 00:57:04.285
if all of these different
possibilities could live.

00:57:04.285 --> 00:57:05.910
I don't see these
things in contention.

00:57:05.910 --> 00:57:08.310
I think you can have
something kind of raw

00:57:08.310 --> 00:57:09.900
at the middle of an
interglot, but it

00:57:09.900 --> 00:57:13.050
can be adapted for
kind of whatever

00:57:13.050 --> 00:57:17.070
the constraints, or
needs, or availabilities

00:57:17.070 --> 00:57:20.392
of where that science needs
to be kind of translated.

00:57:25.620 --> 00:57:28.260
We're running toward the
hour, but I want quickly

00:57:28.260 --> 00:57:30.480
to give you guys an
opportunity to ask

00:57:30.480 --> 00:57:34.700
each other
super-quick questions.

00:57:34.700 --> 00:57:36.480
I mean, what kinds
of things came up

00:57:36.480 --> 00:57:38.340
for you that we
haven't surfaced yet,

00:57:38.340 --> 00:57:43.560
just so we can offer something.

00:57:43.560 --> 00:57:44.210
Go ahead.

00:57:44.210 --> 00:57:44.710
I mean--

00:57:44.710 --> 00:57:47.310
Yeah, just for
me-- oh, go ahead.

00:57:47.310 --> 00:57:49.850
No, no, go ahead.

00:57:49.850 --> 00:57:53.570
Well, just for me,
doubling back to looking

00:57:53.570 --> 00:57:59.510
at Rosa's presentation and
reading Rosa's work, just

00:57:59.510 --> 00:58:03.200
how the basic question of
how we're even imagining

00:58:03.200 --> 00:58:05.510
the relationship
between the information,

00:58:05.510 --> 00:58:08.190
and often in the individual
sense of your work,

00:58:08.190 --> 00:58:12.020
Rosa, how that comports
very neatly over to biology.

00:58:12.020 --> 00:58:15.140
I mean, we aren't even thinking
about the problem of what

00:58:15.140 --> 00:58:18.620
a phenotype is correctly,
which is why we're never,

00:58:18.620 --> 00:58:22.460
ever getting after very neat
and clean genetic answers

00:58:22.460 --> 00:58:24.053
for even diseases.

00:58:24.053 --> 00:58:25.970
I mean, we're getting
interesting information,

00:58:25.970 --> 00:58:27.525
but it's not as
simple as we thought.

00:58:27.525 --> 00:58:29.150
And it's in part
because we're thinking

00:58:29.150 --> 00:58:31.130
about the problem
in a broken way,

00:58:31.130 --> 00:58:34.610
not unlike the way that you've
constructed your resolution

00:58:34.610 --> 00:58:37.860
kind of problem in the
visual and in other contexts.

00:58:37.860 --> 00:58:40.190
So that's just something I
kind of highlighted that I

00:58:40.190 --> 00:58:41.860
thought really was interesting.

00:58:41.860 --> 00:58:42.890
I'll stop there now.

00:58:42.890 --> 00:58:46.700
But I think we have to
give props to Stefan here,

00:58:46.700 --> 00:58:48.950
because I think what he
did is he created a really

00:58:48.950 --> 00:58:53.570
nice panel in which Priya, me,
and you, we kind of have this--

00:58:53.570 --> 00:58:56.330
I wouldn't say like
"flow" between us,

00:58:56.330 --> 00:59:01.470
but of course, Priya is
researching a particular thing.

00:59:01.470 --> 00:59:03.890
And so she's finding,
trying to find models,

00:59:03.890 --> 00:59:05.780
whereas you're like
reverse engineering,

00:59:05.780 --> 00:59:08.930
also, to see how things
have become something.

00:59:08.930 --> 00:59:14.810
And then me personally, I'm
trying to establish this tool

00:59:14.810 --> 00:59:16.970
to see how we can
make things, or how

00:59:16.970 --> 00:59:21.800
you can make maybe problems
and redefine ways of seeing.

00:59:21.800 --> 00:59:25.340
So I see that that's
how that came about.

00:59:25.340 --> 00:59:26.780
Yeah.

00:59:26.780 --> 00:59:29.120
So thank you, Stefan.

00:59:29.120 --> 00:59:30.504
Thank you.

00:59:30.504 --> 00:59:31.290
Yeah.

00:59:31.290 --> 00:59:32.150
Thank you so much.

00:59:32.150 --> 00:59:34.970
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:59:34.970 --> 00:59:37.280
I mean, I guess
the only question

00:59:37.280 --> 00:59:40.310
that I had was about
how do we-- we are all

00:59:40.310 --> 00:59:43.220
united in our ambition
of what we want, I think,

00:59:43.220 --> 00:59:49.100
for science, society,
culture, and so on.

00:59:49.100 --> 00:59:54.830
But I'm curious about should
we really aim for universals?

00:59:54.830 --> 00:59:56.990
This is the question that I--

00:59:56.990 --> 00:59:59.690
and I would really
like Stefan to weigh

00:59:59.690 --> 01:00:02.780
in as an anthropologist,
where you have looked

01:00:02.780 --> 01:00:07.460
at multiplicity,
and you're not as

01:00:07.460 --> 01:00:11.240
focused on finding the
explanation for something,

01:00:11.240 --> 01:00:11.740
right?

01:00:14.590 --> 01:00:17.110
Oh, that's a difficult question,
Priya, thank you for that.

01:00:19.840 --> 01:00:23.140
Yes, I mean, so much in cultural
anthropology is precisely

01:00:23.140 --> 01:00:25.360
about demonstrating
that things that people

01:00:25.360 --> 01:00:29.830
think of as universals are
parochial and have histories.

01:00:29.830 --> 01:00:32.770
And there's various
ways into that.

01:00:32.770 --> 01:00:35.530
We can think about
histories of color.

01:00:35.530 --> 01:00:38.920
We can think about histories of
genes as holding information.

01:00:38.920 --> 01:00:40.990
And we can unwind
all of those things,

01:00:40.990 --> 01:00:43.780
and unwind the universality
of those claims.

01:00:43.780 --> 01:00:48.100
And we are winding
up the time, I see.

01:00:48.100 --> 01:00:50.470
There's a clock
flashing at me here.

01:00:50.470 --> 01:00:53.690
And I just want to
thank you, all three,

01:00:53.690 --> 01:00:56.980
so much for this incredibly
wonderful conversation,

01:00:56.980 --> 01:00:58.780
and for the superb--

01:00:58.780 --> 01:01:01.570
just absolutely
superb-- presentations

01:01:01.570 --> 01:01:04.390
you recorded for us here
at [? CAST ?] at MIT.

01:01:04.390 --> 01:01:08.360
And just thank you
so much, everybody.

01:01:08.360 --> 01:01:09.860
No, thank you to all of you.

01:01:09.860 --> 01:01:10.360
Thank you.

01:01:10.360 --> 01:01:11.240
That was absolutely--

01:01:11.240 --> 01:01:11.680
Thank you.

01:01:11.680 --> 01:01:12.510
--a real privilege.

01:01:12.510 --> 01:01:13.010
Amazing.

01:01:13.010 --> 01:01:14.360
A pleasure, yeah.

01:01:14.360 --> 01:01:15.050
Amazing.

01:01:15.050 --> 01:01:16.200
Privilege, indeed.

01:01:16.200 --> 01:01:17.586
Thank you.

01:01:17.586 --> 01:01:19.730
And thank you, Stefan.

01:01:19.730 --> 01:01:21.800
